#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d13310c-2b80-5945-ada3-31d886f1ec48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Rate of Change: Word Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "102f480c-0b70-562a-8bb4-30ec6a97cacd" :question/math-academy-id "q-21123" :question/problem "The number of machines infected by a computer virus is represented by the function $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus was first detected. What is the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5t+11$ be the number of machines infected by the computer virus after $t$ days. To find the average rate of growth of $f(t)$ over the first week, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,7]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δt} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(0)}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[4(7)^{3}-2(7)^{2}+5(7)+11]-[4(0)^{3}-2(0)^{2}+5(0)+11]}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1309}{7} \\\\  & =187\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth over the first week is $187$ machines per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "051b5337-7671-599f-a7f3-c3fb064575cc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Interval Over Which the Average Rate of Change is Zero"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9defd366-8d4b-5775-94e7-9eeab1aed32c" :question/math-academy-id "q-69674" :question/problem "Find an interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-1)=f(9)=-4,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-1,9].$"}]}
